WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.685
CARTER MORGAN: Welcome to "Cloud
Security Basics," a new series

00:00:02.685 --> 00:00:04.770
where we explain the
ins and outs of securing

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:06.780
your application
on Google Cloud.

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:07.308
Sound fun?

00:00:07.308 --> 00:00:09.100
Then stick around,
because in this episode,

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:12.037
you'll learn how Google Cloud
protects customers' data.

00:00:12.037 --> 00:00:15.933
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:25.673 --> 00:00:30.570
[DIALING]

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:32.393
BRAD ACTOR: Hello,
cloud detective.

00:00:32.393 --> 00:00:34.560
You may have answered basic
questions about platform

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:37.950
security, but what do you know
about how Google Cloud secures

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:40.530
data inside of its platform?

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:43.980
As you hit send on your keyboard
and send data over the network,

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:47.610
one of my hench people
could intercept that data,

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:50.130
or I could plant malware
inside of your system

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:52.530
to nefariously do my bidding.

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:54.810
What can you do to stop that?

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:57.360
For your sake, I hope you know
the answer to my question.

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:02.210
CARTER MORGAN: OK,
here's what we know.

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:04.480
This comes down to
securing data at rest

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:06.590
and securing data in transit.

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:09.080
When Brad Actor talks
about a malicious service

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:12.067
inside of your system gaining
access to your other services,

00:01:12.067 --> 00:01:14.150
there are a lot of ways
Google protects from that.

00:01:14.150 --> 00:01:16.910
For one, to keep data
private and secure,

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:19.370
Google logically isolates
each customer's data

00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:21.050
from that of other
customers and users,

00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:25.130
even when it's stored on
the same physical server.

00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:27.860
As Google is managing this
for its cloud clients,

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:30.470
it protects them from
employees abusing their access.

00:01:30.470 --> 00:01:32.570
Only a small group
of Google employees

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:34.740
ever have access
to customer data.

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:37.220
Additionally, Google
doesn't scan customer data

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:39.950
for advertisements or
sell it to third parties.

00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:41.930
And then if customers
delete their data,

00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:44.000
Google commits to deleting
it from their systems

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:45.800
within 180 days.

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:49.100
Finally, Google provides tools
that make it easy for customers

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:51.230
to take their data with
them if they ever choose

00:01:51.230 --> 00:01:53.660
to stop using Google services.

00:01:53.660 --> 00:01:55.160
These are just a
few examples of how

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:56.827
Google Cloud protects
customer data that

00:01:56.827 --> 00:01:58.040
lives in Google Cloud.

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:00.470
Let's look at a few more.

00:02:00.470 --> 00:02:04.270
So how does Google Cloud protect
from data being intercepted

00:02:04.270 --> 00:02:06.880
as it's sent over the network?

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:11.330
Before we answer that, let's
talk about data at rest.

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:13.552
When users store their
data in Google Cloud,

00:02:13.552 --> 00:02:15.260
the various Google
Cloud Storage services

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:18.110
can be configured to use keys
from a central key management

00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:20.060
service to encrypt
data before it's

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:22.220
written to physical storage.

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:24.470
Performing encryption
at the application layer

00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:26.402
allows the infrastructure
to isolate itself

00:02:26.402 --> 00:02:27.860
from potential
threats at the lower

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:31.910
levels of storage, such as
a malicious disk firmware.

00:02:31.910 --> 00:02:33.690
Other layers of
protections are also used,

00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:35.950
such as hardware encryption.

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:38.950
But once data leaves
Google's secure server,

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:40.870
customer data must
make several journeys

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:44.910
across devices, known as hops,
across the public internet.

00:02:44.910 --> 00:02:46.660
The number of hops
depends on the distance

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:49.570
between the customer's ISP and
the solution's data center.

00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:52.660
Each additional hop introduces
a new opportunity for data

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:55.420
to be attacked or intercepted.

00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:57.550
Because it's linked to
most ISPs in the world,

00:02:57.550 --> 00:03:00.460
though, Google's global
network improves the security

00:03:00.460 --> 00:03:02.380
of data in transit
by limiting hops

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:04.330
across the public internet.

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:06.860
All traffic is routed through
custom Google front end

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:08.760
servers.

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:11.220
Google front end servers
are used to detect and stop

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:12.870
malicious requests
and distributed

00:03:12.870 --> 00:03:14.610
denial of service attacks.

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:16.668
Additionally, GFE
servers are only

00:03:16.668 --> 00:03:18.960
allowed to communicate with
the control list of servers

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:19.950
internally.

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:23.250
This default deny configuration
prevents GFC servers

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:25.710
from accessing
unintended resources.

00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:27.540
As the data moves
along the network,

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:30.240
Google uses cryptography to
ensure the data is private,

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:32.700
unchanged by any third
party, and is being exchanged

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:34.710
between trusted partners.

00:03:34.710 --> 00:03:36.960
These cryptographic features
are encapsulated inside

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:38.920
of the Google Cloud
RPC mechanisms.

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:40.920
So they are available to
other application layer

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:43.470
protocols, such as HTTP.

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:45.690
This provides application
layer isolation,

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:49.200
and removes dependency on the
security of the network path.

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:50.850
Encrypted interservice
communication

00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:53.250
can remain secure, even
if the network is tapped

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:55.620
or a network device compromised.

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:58.680
On top of that, Google uses
industry standard firewalls

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:00.720
and access control
lists, or ACLs,

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:02.670
to ensure network
segregation, adding

00:04:02.670 --> 00:04:05.430
another layer of protection
to sensitive networks.

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:08.440
Lastly, since Google is
using the same infrastructure

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:11.670
to provide to its users, the
same DDoS and man-in-the-middle

00:04:11.670 --> 00:04:15.490
protections we talked about
previously apply here,

00:04:15.490 --> 00:04:20.381
which means I know the answer
to Brad Actor's question.

00:04:20.381 --> 00:04:21.589
BRAD ACTOR: Hello, detective.

00:04:21.589 --> 00:04:24.020
I see you've been busy and
solved my little question.

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:25.910
Once data is stored in
Google's data centers,

00:04:25.910 --> 00:04:28.130
what's keeping it safe?

00:04:28.130 --> 00:04:28.670
Very good.

00:04:28.670 --> 00:04:30.680
You found the crux
of the argument.

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:32.780
Google has protections
in place to store data

00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:35.540
in transit and data at rest.

00:04:35.540 --> 00:04:37.310
Data stored on
Google's server is only

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:39.620
ever accessed by a
Google employee on a need

00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:41.360
to know basis.

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:43.540
Once data leaves
Google's data centers,

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:45.290
there are a few tools
Google puts in place

00:04:45.290 --> 00:04:47.720
to protect customers' data,
like custom networking

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:49.940
hardware, interservice
encryption,

00:04:49.940 --> 00:04:52.070
and interservice ACLs.

00:04:52.070 --> 00:04:52.970
Very good.

00:04:52.970 --> 00:04:54.740
Very good.

00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:58.020
You've answered today's
question, but are you ready?

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:00.620
Because next time, I want to
see how your platform handles

00:05:00.620 --> 00:05:02.273
physical security.

00:05:02.273 --> 00:05:03.440
CARTER MORGAN: So that's it.

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:06.200
Another episode of
Cloud Security basics.

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:08.840
Next episode will focus on how
Google Cloud secures the data

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:10.100
centers and hardware.

00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:12.680
In the meantime, if you want
to take a deeper dive in how

00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:14.683
Google secures its
users customer data,

00:05:14.683 --> 00:05:16.850
then check out the article
linked in the description

00:05:16.850 --> 00:05:17.578
below.

00:05:17.578 --> 00:05:20.120
Stay tuned for the rest of the
"Cloud Security Basic" series,

00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:21.890
because when it
comes to security,

00:05:21.890 --> 00:05:23.630
you can't let bad actors win.

00:05:23.630 --> 00:05:27.580
[MUSIC PLAYING]

